PF('04 / Playfair cipher',[P('A compact construction for encrypting letter pairs by hand.'),P('Invented by Charles Wheatstone in 1854 and promoted by Lyon Playfair.')],kind='divider',take='Historical attraction: pair substitution without carrying a huge substitution table.')
PF('Why substitute more than one letter?',[P('Single-letter substitution leaves each letter’s frequency directly visible under another label.'),P('Playfair works with <b>digrams</b>: pairs of distinct letters prepared from the message.'),P('Codebooks could replace entire words or instructions, but large books were harder to distribute and protect.')],take='This is polygraphic substitution; Vigenère instead changes a single-letter mapping with position.')
PF('Build the square: normalize the keyword',[C('KEYWORD','PLAYFAIR EXAMPLE'),C('KEEP FIRST OCCURRENCES ONLY','P L A Y F I R E X M'),P('Merge J into I, remove spaces, and scan left to right, skipping any letter already entered.'),P('These ten distinct letters occupy the first two rows of the 5 × 5 square.')])
PF('Build the square: fill the remaining cells',[Two([P('First enter the keyword letters.'),Grid(limit=10)],[P('Then append unused letters in order.'),Grid()])],take='Unused letters: B C D G H K N O Q S T U V W Z. Both parties build the same square.')
PF('Prepare the message, one pair at a time',[T(['Remaining text','Action','Pair emitted'],[['BALLOON','B and A differ; consume both.','BA'],['LLOON','Repeated L: consume first L; insert X.','LX'],['LOON','L and O differ; consume both.','LO'],['ON','O and N differ; consume both.','ON']],[220,480,156]),P('Thus BALLOON becomes BA LX LO ON. Do not consume both repeated L letters when inserting the filler.')])
PF('Preparation conventions and the gold example',[P('If one letter remains, append X. For a repeated or final X, use Q as the alternate filler.'),C('EXAMPLES','X → XQ     XX → XQ XQ'),C('HIDE THE GOLD IN THE TREE STUMP','HI DE TH EG OL DI NT HE TR EX ES TU MP'),P('Only letters within the next proposed pair trigger the repeated-letter rule.')],take='Preparation can lose information; we keep it separate from the reversible pair transformation.')
PF('Rule 1: letters in the same row',[Two([P('1. Locate P and L in row 1.'),P('2. Move each one cell to the right.'),P('3. Preserve their order.'),C('RESULT','PL → LA\nWrap-around: FP → PL')],[Grid('PL')])],take='Grid shading marks the input pair. Decryption moves left instead of right.')
PF('Rule 2: letters in the same column',[Two([P('1. Locate P and I in column 1.'),P('2. Move each one cell down.'),P('3. Wrap from the bottom to the top.'),C('RESULT','PI → IB\nWrap-around: TP → PI')],[Grid('PI')])],take='Decryption moves up; both row and column rules wrap around the square.')
PF('Rule 3: opposite rectangle corners',[Two([P('H is at (row 3, column 5).'),P('I is at (row 2, column 1).'),P('Keep each input row; exchange the columns.'),C('RESULT','H → B at (3,1)\nI → M at (2,5)\nHI → BM')],[Grid('HI')])],take='The first output stays in the first input’s row; pair order matters.')
PF('Encrypt the prepared message in stages',[T(['Input pair','Relation in the square','Output'],[['HI','Rectangle','BM'],['DE','Same column','OD'],['TH','Rectangle','ZB'],['EG','Rectangle','XD']],[210,436,210]),P('Work through these first four pairs before applying the same rules to the rest.')])
PF('Complete the encryption and reverse it',[C('PREPARED PLAINTEXT','HI DE TH EG OL DI NT HE TR EX ES TU MP'),C('CIPHERTEXT','BM OD ZB XD NA BE KU DM UI XM MO UV IF'),P('For decryption: move left in a row, up in a column, or apply the same rectangle rule.'),P('Check BM → HI and OD → DE using the square.')],source='Standard PLAYFAIR EXAMPLE illustration, also present in the supplied deck.')
PF('Exercise: decrypt with the same square',[Two([C('CIPHERTEXT','RO NC FL WI'),P('Decrypt each pair and name the rule used.'),P('Keep any filler until you have recovered all the prepared letters.')],[Grid()])],sub='Pair exercise • 3 minutes',take='The complete calculation follows on the next slide.')
PF('Solution: show every pair and rule',[T(['Ciphertext','Inverse rule','Prepared plaintext'],[['RO','Rectangle','EN'],['NC','Same column: move up','CR'],['FL','Same row: move left','YP'],['WI','Rectangle','TX']],[210,436,210]),C('RESULT','EN CR YP TX → ENCRYPTX'),P('The intended reading is ENCRYPT, with a final X supplied during preparation.')])
PF('Why exact spelling may not be recoverable',[C('TWO DIFFERENT ORIGINALS','AXA → AX AX\nAA  → AX AX'),P('Both originals produce the same prepared text and therefore the same ciphertext.'),P('Merging I and J also loses information. Do not automatically remove every X from a decryption.')],take='The pair transformation is invertible on prepared pairs; preprocessing need not be invertible.')
PF('Historical usefulness and cryptanalysis',[P('Playfair offered a practical balance between manual operation and more complex substitution; it saw military field use.'),P('Pair and longer language patterns still survive. A computer can decrypt with candidate squares and score the results.'),C('HILL-CLIMBING OUTLINE','square → decrypt → language score\nmodify square → keep improvement → repeat'),P('Random restarts help with local optima. Success depends on the message, language model, and search effort.')],take='Understand the historical design goal before judging it by present-day requirements.')

