TR('Encrypt one block, step by step',[T(['Output position','1','2','3','4','5','6'],[['Read input position']+[str(v) for v in PERM],['Input letter from ATTACK']+[norm('ATTACK')[v-1] for v in PERM]],[250,101,101,101,101,101,101]),C('TWO-BLOCK MESSAGE','ATTACK ATDAWN\n'+perm('ATTACK')+' '+perm('ATDAWN')),P('Every letter appears exactly as often after rearrangement as before it.')])
TR('Construct the inverse permutation',[T(['Original input j','1','2','3','4','5','6'],[['Output position containing j']+[str(v) for v in INV]],[250,101,101,101,101,101,101]),C('READ THE INVERSE','π⁻¹ = [2, 5, 1, 3, 6, 4]\nTAAKTC → ATTACK'),M(r'c_{\pi^{-1}(j)}=p_{\pi(\pi^{-1}(j))}=p_j.')],take='The equality explains why the inverse table undoes encryption.')
TR('Exercise: encrypt, then explain an invariant',[C('PLAINTEXT AND KEY','DEFEND\nπ = [3, 1, 4, 6, 2, 5]'),P('Encrypt the block and recover it using the inverse permutation.'),P('How many keys are possible at block size 6? Which letter statistics are unchanged?')],sub='Individual calculation • 2 minutes',take='Use the declared output-to-input convention, not a memorized alternative.')
TR('Solution and security discussion',[C('SOLUTION','DEFEND → '+perm('DEFEND')+' → DEFEND'),M(r'|S_6|=6!=720,\qquad f_C(a)=f_P(a)\text{ for every letter }a.'),P('For this block size, exhaustive search is small. Candidate rearrangements can also be ranked using language patterns.'),P('With larger blocks there are more keys, but unchanged counts remain an important structural property.')])

